1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,620
Normandy, nineteen forty-four.

2
00:00:02,660 --> 00:00:05,420
This American soldier is
about to cross one field.

3
00:00:06,220 --> 00:00:08,780
He carries a rifle, a
shovel, and one day of food.

4
00:00:09,490 --> 00:00:10,520
But he is not alone.

5
00:00:11,260 --> 00:00:13,960
Behind him stands a line of
people no war movie shows.

6
00:00:14,200 --> 00:00:15,480
How long is that line?

7
00:00:16,200 --> 00:00:18,160
By the end, you'll know the exact number.

8
00:00:18,900 --> 00:00:20,380
Let's start with something simple.

9
00:00:21,200 --> 00:00:24,160
Breakfast. At the front, our
soldier often eats a K ration.

10
00:00:24,530 --> 00:00:27,040
A small waxed cardboard box of food.

11
00:00:27,600 --> 00:00:29,060
Three boxes make one day.

12
00:00:29,720 --> 00:00:31,360
Breakfast, dinner, and supper.

13
00:00:31,520 --> 00:00:34,860
Together, they hold about two thousand
eight hundred and thirty calories.

14
00:00:35,960 --> 00:00:38,100
Each box was planned
down to small details.

15
00:00:38,900 --> 00:00:41,360
Besides the food, there were
a few cigarettes, chewing gum,

16
00:00:41,400 --> 00:00:42,600
and toilet paper.

17
00:00:43,480 --> 00:00:46,080
Somebody, in an office far
away, decided all of that.

18
00:00:46,730 --> 00:00:47,800
That sounds like plenty.

19
00:00:48,560 --> 00:00:53,400
But it isn't. A man who digs holes and
carries ammunition all day burns far more.

20
00:00:53,840 --> 00:00:57,220
So front-line soldiers were
hungry almost all the time.

21
00:00:57,980 --> 00:00:59,320
Now ask a strange question.

22
00:01:00,140 --> 00:01:02,060
Who made this box? Not an army cook.

23
00:01:02,220 --> 00:01:05,600
A factory, thousands of miles
away, back in the United States.

24
00:01:06,840 --> 00:01:07,840
Farmers grew the food.

25
00:01:08,660 --> 00:01:09,880
Factory workers packed it.

26
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:11,380
Railroad crews hauled it to a port.

27
00:01:11,420 --> 00:01:14,200
Dockworkers lifted it onto a ship.

28
00:01:14,860 --> 00:01:18,760
So before our soldier takes one bite,
dozens of people have already touched his

29
00:01:18,800 --> 00:01:19,800
breakfast.

30
00:01:20,220 --> 00:01:21,560
But food is the easy part.

31
00:01:21,600 --> 00:01:22,700
Food does not explode.

32
00:01:22,740 --> 00:01:24,240
And food does not sink.

33
00:01:25,080 --> 00:01:28,420
Everything this soldier uses
has to cross the Atlantic Ocean.

34
00:01:29,120 --> 00:01:31,920
That is more than three
thousand miles of open water.

35
00:01:32,640 --> 00:01:36,660
But German submarines, the U-boats,
were hunting the ships that carried it.

36
00:01:37,160 --> 00:01:41,080
Every ship that went down took bullets,
food, and sometimes men with it.

37
00:01:41,620 --> 00:01:43,000
So America needed ships.

38
00:01:43,580 --> 00:01:46,440
Not a few. Thousands. The
answer was the Liberty ship.

39
00:01:46,760 --> 00:01:51,280
A simple, slow cargo ship, built
almost like a car on an assembly line.

40
00:01:52,160 --> 00:01:56,240
Many of the welders in those shipyards
had never held a torch before the war.

41
00:01:57,180 --> 00:01:58,460
Plenty of them were women.

42
00:01:58,660 --> 00:02:01,180
They learned in weeks a job
that used to take years.

43
00:02:02,060 --> 00:02:05,860
American shipyards built two thousand
seven hundred and ten of them.

44
00:02:06,560 --> 00:02:07,660
One of them, the Robert E.

45
00:02:07,700 --> 00:02:11,300
Peary, went from keel to launch
in four days and fifteen hours.

46
00:02:12,210 --> 00:02:13,420
But a ship needs a crew.

47
00:02:14,020 --> 00:02:18,140
The men who sailed these cargo ships
were mostly civilian merchant mariners.

48
00:02:19,080 --> 00:02:22,280
Not soldiers. Yet their job was
one of the deadliest at sea.

49
00:02:23,020 --> 00:02:24,260
A ship, in less than a week.

50
00:02:24,840 --> 00:02:26,740
That sounds like the problem is solved.

51
00:02:26,780 --> 00:02:28,680
But a ship only gets you to the coast.

52
00:02:29,560 --> 00:02:32,720
And by the end of summer, our
soldier was nowhere near the coast.

53
00:02:33,520 --> 00:02:37,660
By late August nineteen forty-four, the
Allied armies had raced across France.

54
00:02:38,460 --> 00:02:40,720
The front was hundreds of
miles from the beaches.

55
00:02:40,760 --> 00:02:43,700
And the French railways had
been wrecked by bombing.

56
00:02:44,600 --> 00:02:46,260
So the Army did something simple.

57
00:02:46,980 --> 00:02:50,880
It used trucks. Thousands of trucks,
driving day and night on one-way roads

58
00:02:50,920 --> 00:02:52,360
closed to everyone else.

59
00:02:53,140 --> 00:02:54,720
They called it the Red Ball Express.

60
00:02:55,460 --> 00:02:58,560
Most of those trucks were the
two-and-a-half-ton workhorse the soldiers

61
00:02:58,600 --> 00:03:00,180
called the deuce and a half.

62
00:03:00,960 --> 00:03:03,700
Each one was loaded far
beyond what it was built for.

63
00:03:04,380 --> 00:03:08,280
On August twenty-ninth, nineteen
forty-four, the Red Ball set its record.

64
00:03:09,120 --> 00:03:13,280
Five thousand nine hundred and fifty-eight
vehicles carried over twelve thousand tons

65
00:03:13,320 --> 00:03:14,320
forward.

66
00:03:14,900 --> 00:03:15,900
In a single day.

67
00:03:16,520 --> 00:03:19,320
About three out of four of those
drivers were Black soldiers.

68
00:03:20,120 --> 00:03:22,160
They served in a segregated army.

69
00:03:22,300 --> 00:03:24,600
They drove for days
with very little sleep.

70
00:03:25,400 --> 00:03:27,160
Some drivers fell asleep at the wheel.

71
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:29,700
Trucks broke down by the thousands.

72
00:03:29,860 --> 00:03:33,600
But the convoys kept rolling, because the
front was screaming for one thing above

73
00:03:33,640 --> 00:03:34,640
all.

74
00:03:35,100 --> 00:03:36,280
There was a cruel twist.

75
00:03:36,940 --> 00:03:40,400
The longer the road got, the more
gasoline the trucks themselves burned.

76
00:03:41,280 --> 00:03:44,400
A big share of the fuel was
used up just delivering fuel.

77
00:03:45,200 --> 00:03:46,200
Gasoline.

78
00:03:46,620 --> 00:03:48,140
And even this was not enough.

79
00:03:48,340 --> 00:03:52,000
At the end of August, General Patton's
tanks ground to a halt near the Meuse

80
00:03:52,040 --> 00:03:53,040
river.

81
00:03:53,520 --> 00:03:56,320
Not because of the Germans.
Because the fuel ran out.

82
00:03:56,980 --> 00:03:58,540
So here is our first big clue.

83
00:03:58,960 --> 00:04:02,360
Fighting a war is not mostly
fighting. It is moving things.

84
00:04:03,180 --> 00:04:05,620
And moving things takes
people. Lots of people.

85
00:04:06,340 --> 00:04:08,040
But soldiers do not run on gasoline.

86
00:04:08,920 --> 00:04:12,560
They run on hope. And at the front,
hope usually arrived in an envelope.

87
00:04:13,400 --> 00:04:15,060
Millions of letters crossed the ocean.

88
00:04:15,760 --> 00:04:19,700
But paper is heavy. Every sack of mail on
a ship was space that could have carried

89
00:04:19,740 --> 00:04:20,940
shells or food.

90
00:04:21,790 --> 00:04:23,000
So the Army got clever.

91
00:04:23,960 --> 00:04:26,300
Letters were photographed
onto tiny rolls of film.

92
00:04:26,880 --> 00:04:29,100
They called it Victory Mail, or V mail.

93
00:04:29,700 --> 00:04:34,280
Thirty-seven mail bags of ordinary letters
shrank into one single bag of film.

94
00:04:35,180 --> 00:04:39,440
On the other side of the ocean, the film
was printed back onto paper and delivered.

95
00:04:40,280 --> 00:04:44,420
For a nineteen-year-old in a muddy hole,
that letter could be the only proof the

96
00:04:44,460 --> 00:04:46,500
world back home still existed.

97
00:04:47,780 --> 00:04:50,420
Mothers wrote about the weather.
Sisters wrote about weddings.

98
00:04:51,380 --> 00:04:52,560
Nobody wrote about the war.

99
00:04:53,190 --> 00:04:56,680
Which means that somewhere behind the
front, soldiers spent their whole war

100
00:04:56,720 --> 00:04:59,840
printing, sorting, and
delivering letters to strangers.

101
00:05:01,060 --> 00:05:02,780
Nobody makes movies about them.

102
00:05:02,820 --> 00:05:05,320
But every man at the front
waited for their work.

103
00:05:06,030 --> 00:05:07,600
Now the hardest moment of the day.

104
00:05:08,300 --> 00:05:09,520
Our soldier is wounded.

105
00:05:09,560 --> 00:05:12,400
Not badly, but he cannot
walk. What happens next?

106
00:05:13,150 --> 00:05:14,540
First, a medic reaches him.

107
00:05:15,140 --> 00:05:16,440
He has no hospital.

108
00:05:16,480 --> 00:05:18,680
Just a canvas bag and his own two hands.

109
00:05:19,480 --> 00:05:21,720
His job is simple and brutal. Buy time.

110
00:05:22,340 --> 00:05:23,860
Litter bearers carry him back.

111
00:05:24,440 --> 00:05:26,660
A battalion aid station checks him.

112
00:05:26,860 --> 00:05:30,840
An ambulance drives him to a field
hospital, where surgeons go to work.

113
00:05:31,900 --> 00:05:33,040
Speed mattered more than anything.

114
00:05:33,840 --> 00:05:37,960
A wounded man who reached a surgeon
quickly had a far better chance of living.

115
00:05:38,720 --> 00:05:40,720
So the whole chain was
built to save minutes.

116
00:05:41,460 --> 00:05:43,220
Some wounded men went even further.

117
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:46,040
Back to England. Some all the way home.

118
00:05:46,160 --> 00:05:49,300
Every step of that trip is handled
by a different group of people.

119
00:05:50,240 --> 00:05:51,580
So let's count who we have met.

120
00:05:52,300 --> 00:05:53,660
Farmers. Factory workers.

121
00:05:53,740 --> 00:05:55,700
Shipbuilders. Sailors. Truck drivers.

122
00:05:55,740 --> 00:05:57,460
Mail clerks. Medics. Surgeons.

123
00:05:58,500 --> 00:06:00,620
How many of them stand behind one soldier?

124
00:06:01,340 --> 00:06:03,580
To answer that, the Army
used a strange word.

125
00:06:04,300 --> 00:06:06,300
A slice. Not of bread. A division slice.

126
00:06:06,960 --> 00:06:08,720
A division is a big army unit.

127
00:06:09,340 --> 00:06:13,080
In nineteen forty-four, an American
infantry division had a little over

128
00:06:13,120 --> 00:06:15,220
fourteen thousand men on paper.

129
00:06:16,140 --> 00:06:17,660
But planners noticed something.

130
00:06:18,420 --> 00:06:21,520
Every division in the line
needed many more men behind it,

131
00:06:21,560 --> 00:06:23,160
in the same theater of war.

132
00:06:24,060 --> 00:06:25,100
Just to keep it moving.

133
00:06:25,820 --> 00:06:28,420
The division, plus its share
of everyone supporting it,

134
00:06:28,460 --> 00:06:29,820
is the division slice.

135
00:06:30,780 --> 00:06:34,400
And the slice was much bigger than
the division itself. How much bigger?

136
00:06:35,180 --> 00:06:36,180
Hold that thought.

137
00:06:36,720 --> 00:06:39,560
Because first, the Army hit
a problem nobody expected.

138
00:06:40,380 --> 00:06:44,260
By late nineteen forty-four, America
had the biggest army in its history.

139
00:06:45,060 --> 00:06:47,200
Around eight million
people in the Army alone.

140
00:06:47,850 --> 00:06:51,140
But in Europe, generals were
running out of one kind of soldier.

141
00:06:52,060 --> 00:06:54,400
Not drivers. Not cooks.
Not clerks. Riflemen.

142
00:06:55,240 --> 00:06:58,860
Rifle companies were losing men
faster than new ones could arrive.

143
00:06:59,660 --> 00:07:03,740
Over months of fighting, some infantry
divisions lost more men in total than they

144
00:07:03,780 --> 00:07:05,080
had on the first day.

145
00:07:05,960 --> 00:07:07,880
So the Army pulled men from other jobs.

146
00:07:08,660 --> 00:07:12,460
Anti-aircraft gunners, clerks, and air
force trainees were sent to infantry

147
00:07:12,500 --> 00:07:14,300
training and handed rifles.

148
00:07:15,270 --> 00:07:19,800
In late December nineteen forty-four, the
Army even asked Black service troops to

149
00:07:19,840 --> 00:07:21,140
volunteer for combat.

150
00:07:22,440 --> 00:07:25,020
Thousands stepped forward. Many
sergeants gave up their stripes,

151
00:07:25,060 --> 00:07:26,640
just for the chance to fight.

152
00:07:27,500 --> 00:07:30,760
About two thousand two hundred of
them went to the front in thirty-seven

153
00:07:30,800 --> 00:07:31,800
platoons.

154
00:07:32,580 --> 00:07:34,380
So here is the real question.

155
00:07:34,580 --> 00:07:37,480
How can an army of eight
million run out of riflemen?

156
00:07:38,300 --> 00:07:39,300
Let's do the math.

157
00:07:39,880 --> 00:07:42,060
Our soldier is in a rifle company.

158
00:07:46,160 --> 00:07:47,820
A rifle company had about one hundred and
ninety-three men. An infantry division had

159
00:07:47,860 --> 00:07:49,180
twenty-seven of them.

160
00:07:49,700 --> 00:07:53,700
Twenty-seven times one hundred and
ninety-three is about five thousand two

161
00:07:53,740 --> 00:07:54,780
hundred men.

162
00:07:55,680 --> 00:07:57,300
Out of fourteen thousand.

163
00:07:57,340 --> 00:08:01,080
So even inside a division, most
men were not in rifle companies.

164
00:08:01,970 --> 00:08:03,160
Now zoom out to Europe.

165
00:08:03,760 --> 00:08:08,460
In June nineteen forty-five, each American
division there came with a slice of about

166
00:08:08,500 --> 00:08:10,900
thirty-five thousand five hundred men.

167
00:08:11,870 --> 00:08:14,260
Divide the slice by the
men in rifle companies.

168
00:08:15,160 --> 00:08:16,760
The answer is almost seven.

169
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:21,080
Almost seven Americans in Europe for
every one man in a rifle company.

170
00:08:22,080 --> 00:08:24,620
Six other men in the
theater for every rifleman.

171
00:08:25,740 --> 00:08:28,780
Drivers, engineers, gunners,
mechanics, cooks, clerks, medics.

172
00:08:29,560 --> 00:08:31,480
All of them needed, all of them working.

173
00:08:31,720 --> 00:08:33,940
But only one of them
walks across the field.

174
00:08:34,720 --> 00:08:35,720
But we are not done.

175
00:08:36,160 --> 00:08:40,040
Add the air force, the training camps,
and everyone in uniform back home.

176
00:08:41,100 --> 00:08:43,100
Divide the whole Army by
its eighty-nine divisions.

177
00:08:43,960 --> 00:08:45,840
That is over ninety thousand men each.

178
00:08:46,460 --> 00:08:48,460
Divide that by the men in rifle companies.

179
00:08:49,260 --> 00:08:52,320
About eighteen. For every
soldier walking toward the enemy,

180
00:08:52,360 --> 00:08:55,400
seventeen others in
uniform stood behind him.

181
00:08:56,160 --> 00:08:59,740
And that is before we count a
single farmer or factory worker.

182
00:09:00,550 --> 00:09:01,620
This was not waste.

183
00:09:02,240 --> 00:09:07,040
It is why the American soldier usually had
food, ammunition, and letters from home.

184
00:09:07,940 --> 00:09:09,260
But the system had a price.

185
00:09:09,800 --> 00:09:12,240
Infantrymen were only a
small part of the Army.

186
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:16,260
Yet of every four Army soldiers
killed in ground combat,

187
00:09:16,340 --> 00:09:18,640
about three were infantrymen.

188
00:09:18,680 --> 00:09:21,960
A tiny tip on a giant spear,
taking almost every hit.

189
00:09:22,630 --> 00:09:26,360
So the next time you watch a war movie,
remember the line behind the soldier.

190
00:09:26,400 --> 00:09:30,640
Seventeen people working so
one man could cross that field.

191
00:09:31,460 --> 00:09:33,600
And one man, carrying
the war for all of them.

192
00:09:34,380 --> 00:09:36,240
Germany built its army very differently.

193
00:09:37,040 --> 00:09:40,340
It invaded the Soviet Union with
hundreds of thousands of horses.

194
00:09:40,840 --> 00:09:41,840
Why?

195
00:09:42,020 --> 00:09:43,280
That is the next video.

196
00:09:43,420 --> 00:09:44,940
Subscribe so you don't miss it.
